Our Operations Are Uncertain and Involve Substantial Costs and Risks

Exploration, drilling and production of oil, gas and NGLs involve numerous risks that are often difficult to predict and which cannot be completely eliminated. These risks include the risk that we will not recover our investment in the wells we drill.

Our exploration and development activities involve numerous risks associated with the inherent uncertainty of oil and gas exploration and development, including the following:

• significant investment costs to identify, evaluate, drill and complete wells and establish or enhance production;

• the risk that the wells we drill will be unproductive or have a shorter productive life than we anticipated;

• the risk that the operational performance of our wells may be lower than expected due to factors such as reservoir characteristics, mechanical or operational issues, or other factors beyond our control;

• the risk of loss of production, loss of reserves, or loss of information and data due to natural disasters, mechanical failures, cyber incidents, vandalism, terrorism, or other unforeseen events;

• risks associated with our dependence on third-party service providers, including the availability, reliability and performance of their products and services, and the potential for disruptions in their supply chains or operations;

• exposure to litigation and regulatory actions related to our operations, including environmental, safety and other compliance matters;

• exposure to cost inflation, supply chain disruptions, and other market conditions that may increase our operating and capital costs; and

• other risks and uncertainties relating to our business and industry.

We face competitive pressures that may adversely affect our ability to acquire, develop and operate oil and gas properties. Our ability to acquire additional reserves and replace our production depends on a variety of factors, including our access to capital, the availability of attractive opportunities and the competitive environment in the oil and gas industry. We compete with other companies for the acquisition of properties, leases and other interests, and for equipment, services and supplies. Some of our competitors have substantially greater financial, technical and other resources than we do and may be better positioned to compete for opportunities and resources. As a result, we may be at a competitive disadvantage in acquiring and developing properties and in operating our assets.

We also face risks associated with our dependence on third-party infrastructure, including pipelines, processing facilities and transportation networks. Disruptions in these systems, whether due to operational issues, weather events, cyber incidents or other causes, could limit our ability to deliver production to market, increase costs or otherwise adversely affect our business and results of operations.

In addition, our business is subject to various risks associated with the regulatory environment in which we operate. Changes in laws, regulations or governmental policies could impose new or more stringent requirements on our operations, increase our costs or otherwise adversely affect our business. We regularly monitor regulatory developments and assess their potential impact on our business and operations. However, we cannot predict with certainty how changes in laws, regulations or policies may affect our business or industry.

 Risks Related to Our International Operations
We operate in other countries and, as a result, are subject to certain political, economic, competitive and other risks.

Our operations in jurisdictions outside the U.S. are subject to various risks inherent in foreign operations. These risks include, among other risks:

• increases in taxes and governmental royalties;

• additional and potentially unfamiliar laws and policies governing the operations of foreign-based companies and changes in such laws and policies;

• loss of revenue, loss of or damage to equipment, property and other assets and interruption of operations as a result of expropriation, nationalization, acts of terrorism, war, civil unrest and other political risks;

• unilateral or forced renegotiation, modification or nullification of existing contracts with governmental entities;

• difficulties enforcing our rights against a governmental agency because of the doctrine of sovereign immunity and foreign sovereignty over international operations;

• competition from companies that have established strategic long-term positions or have strong governmental relationships in the foreign jurisdictions in which we operate; and

• currency restrictions or exchange rate fluctuations.

Our international operations may also be adversely affected by U.S. laws and policies affecting foreign trade and taxation, including tariffs or trade or other economic sanctions; modifications to, or withdrawal from, international trade treaties; and U.S. laws with respect to participation in boycotts that are not supported by the U.S. government. The realization of any of these factors could materially and adversely affect our business, financial condition and results of operations.
